wed\" error. Instead of repeatedly attempting to flash, a direct transition to `U-Boot Fastboot` (`PID_0D02`) via `fastboot reboot bootloader` appears necessary when `fastbootd` is detected. This would streamline the process significantly. The user's input f\n<truncated 1426 bytes>\n use `fastboot reboot bootloader`), preventing exits from Fastboot before firmware injection. The overall process will now be fully automatic with direct firmware injection.\n\n\nThe automated process scans for a USB drive and firmware, updating relevant files if the drive is found. It then enters Fastboot mode, detecting and directly transitioning from \"Recovery Fastbootd\" to \"U-Boot Fastboot\" via `fastboot reboot bootloader`, skipping the previous failed attempts to flash in fastbootd. This step is critical as it bypasses an earlier inefficient method. This ensures faster access to the required Fastboot environment.\n\n\nAfter detecting `U-Boot Fastboot`, the script executes a complete unlocking sequence. This includes disabling the watchdog, checking unlock ability, unlocking critical partitions, performing a standard unlock, setting environment variables, and saving the environment. The sequence concludes with a `fastboot reboot bootloader` command to ensure the unlock process takes effect, mirroring the successful unlock procedure found in working batch scripts. It includes polling the device during the reboot.\n\n\nThe process now transitions seamlessly through USB burn mode and identifies the U-Boot Fastboot state. Automatic firmware injection is triggered immediately in the bootloader. A robust firmware flashing procedure has been implemented. The injection process handles all required partitions, preserves sensitive data like Widevine and MAC/Serial, provides clean progress output, and optionally injects the Xview UI APKs through ADB if available. The process ends with success banner verification.\n\n\nThe menu is condensed to the essential steps for clarity and direct execu